 ],
 "literal": "Those who say 'bear the passion!', do they / not know that? I wonder, are they of such strength? / We, if we do not see our lover, / with a heart of dense distress increased, of the great water's / rock-dashing small foam like, / slowly, slowly, we will cease to exist.",
 "translation": "Those who say, 'Bear your passion!'—do they not know what it is, or are they possessed of such great strength? If we do not see our lover, with a heart filled with growing distress, like the tiny foam beaten against the rocks by the great ocean, slowly, slowly, we will cease to exist.",
 "modern": "காமத்தைத் தாங்கிக் கொள் என்று சொல்பவர்கள், அதை அறியாதவர்களோ? அல்லது அவ்வளவு வலிமை உடையவர்களோ? நாம் நம் காதலரைக் காணவில்லை என்றால், மிகுந்த துன்பம் பெருகிய நெஞ்சத்தோடு, பெரிய கடலின் பாறையில் மோதும் சிறு நுரையைப் போல, மெல்ல மெல்ல இல்லாமல் போய்விடுவோம்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her confidante (or to herself) in deep distress during the hero's absence. She questions the advice of onlookers who casually tell her to control and endure her passion, wondering if they lack understanding or possess superhuman strength. She describes her fragile state of mind, filled with growing grief because she cannot see her lover. She compares her life to the tiny foam created when waves crash against the rocks of the vast ocean. Just as that delicate foam is slowly and inevitably dissolved by the relentless waves, her existence is gradually fading away. This poignant image of the seashore (Neytal tiṇai) perfectly captures the themes of separation, despair, and the fragility of life.",
